import Base from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>Multiple-Choice Strategy</h1>

<p>
    42 questions, 90 minutes, 55% of your score, four choices each. That is
    just over two minutes a question, and the section is front-loaded with
    code reading. You have answered hundreds of MC-format questions this
    year: every Socratic checkpoint, every quiz, every bug hunt. This page is
    about pacing and elimination, the two skills that turn knowledge into
    points.
</p>

<h2>The Six Archetypes</h2>

<table>
    <tr><th>Archetype</th><th>Looks like</th><th>Your move</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Trace it</td><td>"What is printed?" with a 5-15 line method</td>
        <td>Variable table on paper. Never in your head. Two minutes is
        plenty IF you write.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Predict the error</td><td>"Which line causes the compile
        error?" or "why does this fail?"</td><td>Types first, then bounds,
        then semicolons. The error is always a type or an edge.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Complete the code</td><td>A blank in a loop or condition</td>
        <td>Write the condition in words, then translate. The options are
        three wrong translations of the same English.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Value of a call</td><td>"What does expr return?" on String/Math/
        ArrayList methods</td><td>JQR. 15 seconds. Verify signature, then
        apply. Watch set/remove return values.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Count the steps</td><td>"How many times does the loop body
        execute?"</td><td>N+1 trap, off-by-one, nested = product. Write the
        first three iterations and the last one.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Responsibility</td><td>A data-ethics scenario (Unit 4.1)</td>
        <td>Front page test. Which option would you defend in the
        headline?</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Elimination Math (four choices changes everything)</h2>

<ol>
    <li>One choice is usually absurd on its face (a type mismatch, a syntax
        error as "output"). Cross it: 25% becomes 33%.</li>
    <li>One choice is the classic misconception (the answer you get by
        forgetting the -1 in substring, or using == for Strings). If you know
        the traps, cross it: 33% becomes 50%.</li>
    <li>Now choose between two with a reason. If you have no reason after 30
        seconds: mark, skip, move. Two minutes is the budget; sunk minutes
        are sunk points elsewhere.</li>
</ol>

<div class="callout trap">
    <strong>The classic misconception options, memorized:</strong>
    substring's end index is EXCLUSIVE. == is not .equals. Integer division
    truncates. Math.random() never returns 1.0. list.remove returns the
    removed element. (int) casts truncate, never round. grid.length is rows.
    If an answer option contradicts one of these, it is the decoy. Cross it
    with confidence.
</div>

<h2>The Timing Plan</h2>

<table>
    <tr><th>Minute</th><th>State</th><th>Action</th></tr>
    <tr><td>0</td><td>Section I begins</td><td>First pass: answer every
        question you can in under 90 seconds. Mark the rest.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>45</td><td>Halfway</td><td>You should have ~25 answered. If
        fewer, raise your skip threshold: mark more aggressively.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>70</td><td>Second pass</td><td>Work marked questions with a
        trace. Write the variable table. Earn the 2-minute points.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>85</td><td>Last sweep</td><td>Every remaining question gets an
        educated guess. NO BLANKS. There is no guessing penalty on this
        exam: a 50-50 guess is half a point in expectation.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>90</td><td>Done</td><td>Breathe. Section II is a different
        muscle and you have trained it too.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Trace Discipline: The Table</h2>

<p>Every "what is printed" question gets a variable table on the question
booklet:</p>

<pre><code>//  int i=0, sum=0;  while (sum &lt; 7) { i++; sum += i % 3; }
//  loop trace:
//  iteration | i (after ++) | i%3 | sum | sum&lt;7?
//      1     |      1        |  1  |  1  |  yes
//      2     |      2        |  2  |  3  |  yes
//      3     |      3        |  0  |  3  |  yes
//      4     |      4        |  1  |  4  |  yes
//      ... keep going until the condition fails</code></pre>

<p>Ninety seconds of writing beats ten minutes of re-reading. The students
who run out of time are the students who trace in their heads. Your lab
notebook habit from September is worth real points here.</p>

<h2>Set-Based Questions</h2>

<p>Some questions come in sets: a code block, then 2-3 questions about it.
Read the code once, carefully, annotating as you go (write what each variable
holds). The per-question cost drops fast once the code is understood. If a
set's code is brutal, do its easiest question first: familiarity with the
code grows and the harder ones get cheaper.</p>

<h2>Drill List (clinic material)</h2>

<ul>
    <li>Two-minute trace drills: five trace questions, timer on, table on
        paper. Score yourself on accuracy AND pace.</li>
    <li>Decoy spotting: take any ten MC questions, and for each wrong
        option, name the misconception it represents. If you cannot name it,
        you have found a gap. That gap goes on your clinic list.</li>
    <li>JQR sprint: twenty "value of the call" questions, sheet in hand,
        target 40 seconds each.</li>
</ul>`;
